  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 419 AM CDT Wed Jun 3 2026\n\nApart from low precip chances in our far southern zones, high \npressure at the surface and ridging aloft will keep rain chances \nat a minimum through the remainder of this work week. The plume \nof deeper moisture will be mostly directed from the Eastern Gulf \ninto central Louisiana and over to Texas and the Southern Plains.\nThe drier air will also let nighttime temperatures fall much \neasier. Min temps in the 60s will be more widespread, and even \nsome upper 50s in eastern Mississippi tonight and Thursday night.\n\nThen this weekend into early next week, the ridge will break down\nand shift eastward. The deeper moisture values will rebound into \nour region, and the the effects of upper-level troughing to our \nwest will gradually overspread our region. Resulting chances for \nshowers and thunderstorms look familiar at this point already this\nseason - scattered to numerous, with flow and vorticity across \nthe region continuing chances into overnight hours. The pattern\nwill get more complex during the mid to late week, as a portion of\nthe ridge cuts off over the Northeast US, and a rex block scenario\nsets up somewhere around the Eastern Seaboard. Still expect daily\nprecip chances through the extended period. /NF/",
